tion\".\n\nEdge cases: multiple segments with same priority, need tie break by order.\n\nLet's produce the code.\n\nAlso note: \"dynamicKeysFrom\" means by_name keys come from segment names; we must preserve names exactly.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    segments = case['segments']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    n = len(segments)\n    # Get initial allocations as minimums\n    allocations = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\n    total_alloc = sum(allocations)\n    # Check constraints? Assume valid.\n    # Pre-compute capacities and importance lists\n    capacities = [seg['capacity'] for seg in segments]\n    importance = [seg['importance'] for seg in segments]\n    # While we can still allocate more\n    while total_alloc < budget:\n        best_idx = -1\n        best_score = -1.0\n        for i in range(n):\n            if allocations[i] < capacities[i]:\n                score = importance[i] / (allocations[i] + 1)  # next position\n                # Use > for strict, and if equal, keep earlier (since we iterate in order)\n                if score > best_score:\n                    best_score = score\n                    best_idx = i\n        if best_idx == -1:  # no room\n            break  # but budget may not be fully used; but constraints should avoid.\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\n        total_alloc += 1\n    # Build output\n    by_name = {seg['name']: allocations[i] for i, seg in enumerate(segments)}\n    return {\n        \"allocations\": allocations,\n        \"by_name\": by_name,\n        \"total\": total_alloc\n    }\n\nBut note: The description says \"exact_total_budget\" - we are allocating exactly budget. We should ensure we allocate exactly budget tokens. The while loop does that. However if capacity prevents, we might not reach budget. That would be an invalid case per constraints, but we can stop. The total will reflect actual allocated. That's fine.\n\nNow, produce the JSON string with escaped characters. We'll embed in a 